Z||000000||jhar1987||03-21-2000||10:20 PM||||this thing acts like the timing is wondering! can you adjust on dis? i know i'm down on compression on to center cyl, but it acts like the timing is off. idea's??||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||03-22-2000||07:46 PM||||There is no adjustment for timing.<P>There is a TSB covering code 42 that recomends replacing the PROM onse everyting else, like the crank sensor coils controller and coils, is checked. Bad plugs or wires my also contribute to a 42 problem.<P>You will need to scan the ECM to get the current PROM ID #. You will need to order the correct kit based on this number.||||reg||1
Z||000002||jhar1987||03-22-2000||08:53 PM||||orge could the computer be having trouble say with the coils, and not knowing where the timing should be at? i guess what i want to say is could this cotribute to the odd high low idle and runs good one min. and bad the next?thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||03-23-2000||01:54 PM||||ogre can correct me if I'm wrong but I believe any time the SE light is on for a code 42 the motor will be running in a "default timing" mode not controlled by the ECM. Is the bad running accompanied by the SE light coming on?||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||03-23-2000||03:32 PM||||Any error in the ECM will put the ECM into 1 of 2 "limp" modes. <BR>mode 1 is a closed loop but the ECM only runs from PROM data. It disables the learned data. This is not the same PROM table as warmup mode.<P>mode 2 is open loop. this is the same PROM table as open loop becuase it hasn't warmed up yet.<P>In either mode the engine wont run well. mode 2 is worse than 1. Extended running in mode 2 can ruin the catalyst as it usually runs rich in this mode. Mode 1 doesn't perform well because you loose the fine tuning of the learned data, but usually won't damage anything. <P>I don't believe the coil controller on a DIS motor switches over control like a distributor motor does. A 42 that's not fatal likely puts the ECM in mode 1 failure above.<P>There are a few things that can cause 42...<P>1. the crank sensor is marginal. It's working but it may be giving weak pulses.<P>2. the coil controller is weak. IT could either be not amplifying the cranks sensor or not feeding back to the ECM.<P>3. the wire from the ECM to the coil controller is damaged or has a bad/loose connector.<P>4. the coils, plugs, or plug wires, are failing. This can unbalance the firing of the cylinder pairs and cause wierd loading of the coils or coil controller outputs.<P>The absolute first thing when working on a DIS motor with ignition problems is good plugs and wires. A single bad plug or wire will affect TWO cylinders. <P>Plug gap MUST be correct. DIS is very crabby about this. You can ruin coils if the gaps are wrong. <P>DO NOT EVER use Bosch Platinum or Platinum +4 spark plugs in the DIS duke. Niether of these plugs has held up in the DIS duke.(((If there is anyone that has a set of these plugs that were fried in a DIS Duke I would like to see them. I'm trying to get an answer as to exactly why they fry.)))<P><BR>And then again, there is the TSB. Once every thing else is checked and known good you may need to replace the ECM PROM. In this case you need to use an ECM scanner to read the PROM ID. You can't read this thru the blink codes or from the PROM physical lable. It must be the number coded into the PROM electrically.||||reg||1
